<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-tyrosine degradation I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="TYRFUMCAT-PWY" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; This pathway of L-tyrosine degradation has been characterized in mammals, some bacteria, and the fungi |FRAME: TAX-162425| and |FRAME: TAX-746128| |CITS: [9734339] [7568087] [15262943][19028908]| and reviewed in |CITS: [15581571]| and |CITS: [11567547]|. L-Phenylalanine degradation shares this pathway after its initial hydroxylation to L-tyrosine (see MetaCyc pathway |FRAME: PHENYLALANINE-DEG1-PWY|). |FRAME: TAX-162425| also degrades phenylacetate via 2-hydroxyphenylacetate to homogentisate using this pathway |CITS: [7673153]|. The pathway ends in intermediates of central metabolism, as indicated by the pathway links. Deficiencies of enzymes in this pathway have been associated with several human genetic disorders (as indicated in the comments for the individual enzymes in this pathway). Homogentisate is a central intermediate. In addition to being further catabolized, it can also be a biosynthetic precursor for melanins (in |CITS: [8071207]|), and is used by higher plants in the biosynthesis of plastoquinone and tocopherols (in |CITS: [15157070]| and reviewed in |CITS: [15581571]|) (see MetaCyc pathways |FRAME: PWY-1581| and |FRAME: PWY-1422|). This pathway does not occur in |FRAME:TAX-562| and |FRAME:TAX-590|, which cannot utilize L-tyrosine as a carbon and energy source (McFall, E. and Newman E. G. in |CITS: [COLISAL]|). The pathway is also lacking in the yeast |FRAME:TAX-4932| which uses a limited number of carbon sources for growth, but can use most amino acids as sole nitrogen source (in |CITS: [15933030]|) (see MetaCyc pathways |FRAME: ANAPHENOXI-PWY| and |FRAME: PWY-5079|). A variation of this pathway has been reported in some Gram positive bacteria that can degrade L-tyrosine via 2,5-dihydroxyphenylacetate (homoprotocatechuate), rather than homogentisate, to pyruvate and succinate |CITS: [931949] [20216]|. Another variation has been shown in the gram positive bacterium |FRAME:TAX-1817| sp. 239 which can degrade L-tyrosine to homogentisate via p-hydroxyphenylpyruvate, p-hydroxyphenylacetaldehyde, and p-hydroxyphenylacetate. D- and L-phenylalanine were similarly degraded by this organism via the corresponding non-hydroxylated compounds |CITS: [DEBOER88]|. There is also evidence that the yeast |FRAME:TAX-4952| can degrade tyrosine to homogentisate through these intermediates |CITS: [11472920]|. Among the archaea, however, there is little information regarding aromatic amino acid degradation, even in the well studied hyperthermophile |FRAME:TAX-2261| (in |CITS: [15803651]|), although a preliminary pathway has been proposed |CITS: [8206994]|.</Description>
  <Comment source="Automated Conversion">This GPML file was automatically generated from PlantCyc flatfiles.</Comment>
